WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.517
Real analytical queries often follow a relationship path across more than two tables.

00:00:05.517 --> 00:00:11.462
In this example, we begin with patients and join to admissions using patient ID.

00:00:11.462 --> 00:00:16.587
Then we join admissions to medical staff using the attending staff ID.

00:00:16.587 --> 00:00:19.922
The final row contains patient information, admission information,

00:00:19.922 --> 00:00:21.452
and information about the attending clinician.

00:00:21.452 --> 00:00:27.927
Notice that the second join uses a different key from the first join.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:32.047
We are not connecting tables just because they contain a similar column.

00:00:32.047 --> 00:00:38.777
We are following the documented relationship path: patient to admission, then admission to attending staff.

00:00:38.777 --> 00:00:45.237
The staff join is a LEFT JOIN because we may want to keep the admission even if the

00:00:45.237 --> 00:00:49.457
staff reference is missing. If each attending staff ID points to at most one staff member,

00:00:49.457 --> 00:00:53.327
adding the staff table should not increase the number of admission rows.

00:00:53.327 --> 00:00:56.257
This same design appears in other domains.

00:00:56.257 --> 00:00:59.932
Retail might follow customer to order to payment.

00:00:59.932 --> 00:01:04.227
Education might follow student to registration to course.

00:01:04.227 --> 00:01:10.591
Writing the relationship path before writing the SQL makes complex joins easier to reason about.
